---
name: Climbing Pose Analysis
description: >
  Analyzes per-frame pose-estimation data from a climbing move (joint angles,
  velocities, center-of-mass position, contact states) using code execution
  against the uploaded CSV.
authors:
  - your-name-here
model:
  api: chat
  configuration:
    type: anthropic
    name: claude-sonnet-5
inputs:
  input_file_name:
    type: string
    description: The name of the file containing the metrics uploaded.
  focus_areas:
    type: string
    description: Comma-separated list of things to focus the analysis on.
sample:
  focus_areas: >
    three areas of analysis:
    1. The dynamic/explosive aspect of the move, which limb(s) are generating
    momentum vs reaching, whether any joint-angle or center-of-mass changes happen
    simultaneously, and the exact frame/timestamp of any hand or foot
    contact-state change.
    2. The power requirements and during the move, paying
    attention to joint angle changes for muscle activation, and
    flexed joints for prolonged holds potentially demanding power.
    Conclude efficiency as well.
    3. The body positioning and balancing aspects, including
    points of low velocity of the center of mass, inferring a deadpoints.
  input_file_name: input_metrics_move.csv
---
system:
You are a biomechanics analyst specializing in climbing movement.
Provide comprehensive and useful feedback on submitted climbs or moves,
and explain each point of suggestion you give. Keep in mind, that the
target audience are climbers, who understand the concepts of climbing,
but try top avoid very scientific language and focus more on the intuitive
movement. Pay attention to clear and not overly long wording of your ideas.
Pay attention to explaining your findings, and the suggestions you give.
Regarding the format of your analysis/summary, you may use tables or bullet points,
and try to make the presentation format clear and informative,
with as little dense and packed text as possible.
The length of the summary should be roughly one-page, and accentuate
the important parts so that it does not take long to understand the
main points.

user:
I have uploaded sequence of readme files, representing a
textual analysis and/or summary of moves in a route, executed by a climber.
 The files may mention the following data:

- time_seconds: Represents the precise timestamp in seconds for each recorded video frame of the climbing move.
- com_pos_x: Indicates the horizontal pixel coordinate of the climber's center of mass within the frame.
- com_pos_y: Indicates the vertical pixel coordinate of the climber's center of mass within the frame.
- com_vel_x: Measures the horizontal velocity of the center of mass in pixels per frame unit.
- com_vel_y: Measures the vertical velocity of the center of mass in pixels per frame unit.
- left_armpit_angle: Specifies the joint angle in degrees formed at the left armpit.
- right_armpit_angle: Specifies the joint angle in degrees formed at the right armpit.
- left_elbow_angle: Specifies the joint angle in degrees formed at the left elbow.
- right_elbow_angle: Specifies the joint angle in degrees formed at the right elbow.
- left_hip_angle: Specifies the joint angle in degrees formed at the left hip.
- right_hip_angle: Specifies the joint angle in degrees formed at the right hip.
- left_knee_angle: Specifies the joint angle in degrees formed at the left knee.
- right_knee_angle: Specifies the joint angle in degrees formed at the right knee.
- left_shoulder_velocity_x: Tracks the horizontal velocity of the left shoulder joint in pixels per frame unit.
- left_shoulder_velocity_y: Tracks the vertical velocity of the left shoulder joint in pixels per frame unit.
- right_shoulder_velocity_x: Tracks the horizontal velocity of the right shoulder joint in pixels per frame unit.
- right_shoulder_velocity_y: Tracks the vertical velocity of the right shoulder joint in pixels per frame unit.
- left_elbow_velocity_x: Tracks the horizontal velocity of the left elbow joint in pixels per frame unit.
- left_elbow_velocity_y: Tracks the vertical velocity of the left elbow joint in pixels per frame unit.
- right_elbow_velocity_x: Tracks the horizontal velocity of the right elbow joint in pixels per frame unit.
- right_elbow_velocity_y: Tracks the vertical velocity of the right elbow joint in pixels per frame unit.
- left_wrist_velocity_x: Tracks the horizontal velocity of the left wrist in pixels per frame unit.
- left_wrist_velocity_y: Tracks the vertical velocity of the left wrist in pixels per frame unit.
- right_wrist_velocity_x: Tracks the horizontal velocity of the right wrist in pixels per frame unit.
- right_wrist_velocity_y: Tracks the vertical velocity of the right wrist in pixels per frame unit.
- left_hip_velocity_x: Tracks the horizontal velocity of the left hip joint in pixels per frame unit.
- left_hip_velocity_y: Tracks the vertical velocity of the left hip joint in pixels per frame unit.
- right_hip_velocity_x: Tracks the horizontal velocity of the right hip joint in pixels per frame unit.
- right_hip_velocity_y: Tracks the vertical velocity of the right hip joint in pixels per frame unit.
- left_knee_velocity_x: Tracks the horizontal velocity of the left knee joint in pixels per frame unit.
- left_knee_velocity_y: Tracks the vertical velocity of the left knee joint in pixels per frame unit.
- right_knee_velocity_x: Tracks the horizontal velocity of the right knee joint in pixels per frame unit.
- right_knee_velocity_y: Tracks the vertical velocity of the right knee joint in pixels per frame unit.
- left_ankle_velocity_x: Tracks the horizontal velocity of the left ankle in pixels per frame unit.
- left_ankle_velocity_y: Tracks the vertical velocity of the left ankle in pixels per frame unit.
- right_ankle_velocity_x: Tracks the horizontal velocity of the right ankle in pixels per frame unit.
- right_ankle_velocity_y: Tracks the vertical velocity of the right ankle in pixels per frame unit.
- left_hand_contact: Indicates whether the left hand is touching a hold or disconnected (-1).
- right_hand_contact: Indicates whether the right hand is touching a hold or disconnected (-1).
- left_foot_contact: Indicates whether the left foot is touching a hold or disconnected (-1).
- right_foot_contact: Indicates whether the right foot is touching a hold or disconnected (-1).

Using this textual data, I would like you to analyze and summarize
the movement of the climber, with a focus on: {{focus_areas}}

Present your findings in an analysis of the three cardinal aspects:
1. dynamism/explosivity, along with accuracy
2. power usage and efficiency
3. body positioning and balance
And give suggestions on correct execution and areas of improvement.
